  <Fragment slot="lede">An org chart is a bet, much like other bets in product. Make it a conscious one.</Fragment>

  <h2>The short answer</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the outcome, not the chart. Find the friction between your teams
    and customer value, and organise around removing it. Look past product,
    design and engineering: research, product operations, data and product
    marketing all shape how value ships. And if you are reorganising, write
    down why, share the thinking, and say what you are not touching.
  </p>

  <h2>Treat the org chart as a bet</h2>
  <p>
    An org chart says: "This layout of people is our best bet of how we
    efficiently and repeatably bring products to market and sustain them over
    time." Like any bet, it can be wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    I've worked through about a dozen reorgs, maybe more. People are hard. Org
    structures are one of the big frontiers of what I call the "Great
    Unexamined": the parts of work life we take for granted.
  </p>
  <p>
    It helps to think of an org as a kind of product: a thing that needs
    intentional design to help someone achieve a purpose. So be clear about the
    purpose.
  </p>
  <p>
    There's no perfect structure to find, either. Organisations are organic,
    more like a garden than a building. And like all organic things, they have
    minds of their own.
  </p>

  <h2>Why reorgs often fail</h2>
  <p>
    The forces behind a reorg are often cost savings or mergers and
    acquisitions, and those forces aren't neutral. Reorgs end up as exercises
    in political posturing, or short-term bets instead of long-term outcomes.
    The result is net neutral customer value, at best.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most reorgs start with every intention of improving things. Then they get
    caught in bulky approvals and worries about misalignment. Leaders don't
    share the context and thinking. People end up confused, cynical or anxious
    about their own position.
  </p>
  <p>
    That has a real cost. Reorganisation can lead to
    <a href="https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/02678373.2015.1136710" {...blank}>poor wellbeing for employees</a>,
    in many cases <a href="https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2016/02/160201220120.htm" {...blank}>regardless of job cuts</a>.
    Where reorgs do help, it's mostly
    <a href="https://oshwiki.eu/wiki/Protecting_worker_health_during_restructuring" {...blank}>when people gain autonomy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What a good reorg looks like</h2>
  <p>
    A good reorg begins with a goal statement. Org design is design. It should
    be guided by an intention we're working towards together. Something like:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      We are better able to deliver value internally and externally than
      before, and in more sustainable ways. We achieve this with clearer
      communication, a better shared clarity of purpose at all levels, and a
      more effective way of sharing context and results across teams.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Work backwards from there. A good reorg starts with people and balances
    that with clear organisational needs. It starts with the friction to
    customer value and business outcomes, and realigns around those two poles.
  </p>
  <p>Four questions help with the tradeoffs:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>What are we optimising for by moving people around? To what end?</li>
    <li>What are the outcomes and goals of the reorg?</li>
    <li>Who and what is involved in getting value into the hands of customers? How can we align around that better?</li>
    <li>What are we clearly not touching or doing right now?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    If we take <a href="https://www.atlassian.com/blog/teamwork/what-is-conways-law-acmi" {...blank}>Conway's Law</a>
    seriously, our structure shapes our product. As long as we're making those
    seams invisible to customers, why not ease them for ourselves too? This
    isn't just an HR concern. It's a design decision for product leaders.
  </p>

  <h2>Look beyond the trio</h2>
  <p>
    Modern product management was built on the trio of product, design and
    engineering. A product org at scale needs a lot more: user research,
    product operations, research operations, data and analytics, product
    marketing, and people and talent.
  </p>
  <p>
    A product leader needs an opinion on how to organise all of them into a
    team that ships value reliably. The product leader isn't just responsible
    for good software. They're responsible for the organisation that makes it
    happen.
  </p>

  <h2>Culture comes before structure</h2>
  <p>
    Culture is built from our beliefs, carried through our systems, and ends in
    our behaviours. Move the boxes without touching the beliefs and systems,
    and not much changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="https://rework.withgoogle.com/guides/understanding-team-effectiveness/steps/foster-psychological-safety/" {...blank}>Psychological safety</a>
    is one of the most important ingredients in teams that outperform their
    competition. Yet few leaders know how to cultivate it. It comes from the
    actions we take as leaders.
  </p>
  <p>
    Hiring is where culture gets built on purpose. Define the technical and
    people skills you need, leave little room for bias, and hire for culture
    add, not culture fit.
  </p>

  <h2>When to bring someone in</h2>
  <p>
    If the product org has outgrown its structure, or a reorg is coming and
    nobody senior owns its design, that's a product leadership gap. A
    <a href="/fractional/">fractional product leader</a> can write the goal
    statement, design the structure with the team, and hand it over.
  </p>
